     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi lavaan fellows,<br><br>I want to use the residuals of a latent regression to predict a third variable, that is:<br><br>- Variable 1 predicts Variable 2 <br>- Residuals of Variable 2 (i.e., withouth the influence of Variable 1) and Variable 1 predict Variable 3<br><br>It\r\n is sometimes used in the literature, for example in Wüstenberg et al. \r\n(2012), page 9-10, Figure 5 (see attachment). They used a MPlus syntax \r\nlike this one:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#008\">by</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind11 ind12 ind13</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#008\">by</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind21 ind22 ind23</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F3 </span><span style=\"color:#008\">by</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind31 ind32 ind33</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>F2 on F1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#008\">by</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F2@0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#008\">with</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F1@0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>F3 on F2_residual F1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>I would adapt it to lavaan in this way:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind11 ind12 ind13<br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind21 ind22 ind23<br>F3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ind31 ind32 ind33<br><br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F1<br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F2<br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2<br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1<br><br>F3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F2_residual F1<br></span></div></code></div><br>Would you agree? <br><br>Well, actually my Factor 2 is a higher order factor of two latent factors. \r\nAccording to Brown (2006), page 205, Figure 5.3, and some thoughts about\r\n identification I would model Factor 2 in this way:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ind21 ind22 ind23<br>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ind24 ind25 ind26<br>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~ 1*</span><code><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span></code><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><br><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span> 1*</span></code></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span><code><span><code><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2<br></span></code></span><span style=\"color:#660\"></span><span style=\"color:#066\"></span></code></span></code><br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2<br></span></div></code></div><br>That\r\n\r\n is,  the factor loadings of the latent factors to the higher order \r\nfactor are constrained to equality for identification issues. Still \r\nokay?<br><br>Okay, now there is a problem with the combined syntax:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span># measurement models<br>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>ind21 ind22 ind23<br>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>ind24 ind25 ind26<br>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~ 1*</span><code><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span></code><span></span><br><span><code><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span> 1*</span></code></span><span><code><span><code><span><code><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2<br></span></code></span></code></span></code>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span> NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span> a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>F2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span></span></span><br><code><span style=\"color:#000\">F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2</span></code><br><br><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span><code><span>F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span> ind11 ind12 ind13</span><span><br>F3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span> ind31 ind32 ind33<br></span></code><br><br></span></code># structural models<br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F1<br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F2<br>F2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F2<br>F2_residual </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1<br><br>F3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F2_residual F1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>It produces this error message: <br><br><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\" class=\"gmail_quote\">Error in lavParseModelString(model) : <br>  lavaan ERROR: duplicate model element in: F2~~0*F2<br></blockquote><br>I\r\n understand the problem (F2 ~~1*F2 vs F2~~0*F2) somehow, but I do not \r\nknow a solution. Is there another possibilty to model Factor 2 in a way \r\nthat I can skip F2 ~~1*F2? Or is there another way to use the residuals \r\nof Factor 2?<br><br>Best,<br>André<br><br><br>References: <br><div style=\"line-height:2;padding-left:2em\">\r\n  <div>Brown, T. A. (2006). <i>Confirmatory factor analysis for applied research</i>. New York: Guilford Press.</div>\r\n  <span title=\"url_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fzotero.org%3A2&amp;rft_id=urn%3Aisbn%3A1593852754&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=Confirmatory%20factor%20analysis%20for%20applied%20research&amp;rft.place=New%20York&amp;rft.publisher=Guilford%20Press&amp;rft.aufirst=Timothy%20A.&amp;rft.aulast=Brown&amp;rft.au=Timothy%20A.%20Brown&amp;rft.date=2006&amp;rft.tpages=475&amp;rft.isbn=1593852754\"></span>\r\n</div><div style=\"line-height:2;padding-left:2em\">\r\n  <div>Wüstenberg, S., Greiff, S., &amp; Funke, J. (2012). Complex problem solving — More than reasoning? <i>Intelligence</i>, <i>40</i>, 1–14. doi:10.1016/j.intell.2011.11.003<br><br></div>\r\n  <span title=\"url_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fzotero.org%3A2&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1016%2Fj.intell.2011.11.003&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.atitle=Complex%20problem%20solving%20%E2%80%94%20More%20than%20reasoning%3F&amp;rft.jtitle=Intelligence&amp;rft.volume=40&amp;rft.aufirst=Sascha&amp;rft.aulast=W%C3%BCstenberg&amp;rft.au=Sascha%20W%C3%BCstenberg&amp;rft.au=Samuel%20Greiff&amp;rft.au=Joachim%20Funke&amp;rft.date=2012-01&amp;rft.pages=1-14&amp;rft.spage=1&amp;rft.epage=14&amp;rft.issn=01602896\"></span></div></div>"
    ]
